WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:07.200
This is the nucleus of the galaxy M87 and this is the first ever image of a black hole.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.620
(applause)

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.740
When I saw the first image I said, you know, wow, that's it.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.680
It's a true, like a Eureka moment, in our scientific lives.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.380
We're looking at this... the mystery of nature that has never... uncovered, right, so... (laughs)

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.160
So this is it. We've finally seen it.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.000
Scientists came up with this crazy idea in the 1930s

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.680
and they never imagined that one day we would actually see a black hole.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.160
It's so far only a few blurry splotches of light

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.950
and yet they contain so much information
about the physics of the universe.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.260
I've been dreaming about this for 25 years.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.680
When I saw the first data it was just some weird plot

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.800
and it looked like there could be a ring in there.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.649
I was like oh my god, this is gonna be interesting.

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:02.760
It looks pretty much what we were
expecting which was for a theoretician

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.590
is always an, you know, a dramatic moment
when you have to compare what you spent

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.120
years simulating with what actually
nature is telling you.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.700
This is just like kind of the beginning of being able to have another window

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.700
into what black holes can tell us about our laws and, and physics...

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.300
um, but already we've learned so much, so you know, we didn't know,

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.840
even though we had predicted that if you had

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.750
a black hole that you would see this
ring of light, we didn't know we were

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.840
gonna get that ring of light. And that's you
know what we were kind of testing.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.549
We could have gotten just a blob. And so
seeing that ring and seeing a ring that

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.420
has a size that is consistent with other
measurements that had been done

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.470
completely differently... I think that in
itself just being able to see that ring

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.560
of light, being able to see that that ring
exists, is huge.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:52.079
We are also releasing this data that has, from the event
horizon telescope that were used to

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:57.119
create this image, so whoever out there
has a you know alternative theory of

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.080
gravity can just grab this data and test it.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.880
Well the big goal still is to make an image of the black hole in the centre  of our Milky Way,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.640
because it has a much better determined mass

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.920
and so we can do
much better tests of general relativity.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.720
And we can also have other telescopes
look at it.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.547
There are near-infrared interferometers which see gas going around it

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:26.720
so combining all this information will give us a much better image of how black holes actually work.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:34.100
The picture we have seen today is only a first taste of what the Event Horizon Telescope can deliver.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.820
Already the data they have in the bag can produce a lot more information,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.520
for example about what produces the jets,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.900
these enormous beams of light that we see coming out of the galaxy M87.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.520
Also the team has collected a lot of data about another black hole,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.100
the one at the center of our own galaxy.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.800
And they haven't even started analyzing that, so stay tuned there's a lot more to come

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.520
you

